Z||000000||Carrolles||11-05-2000||09:43 AM||||All of the delay control on my wipers happens in the first few degrees of rotation of the stalk.  Its hard to get the interval set between full on and a few seconds.<P>Anyone have this problem and found a fix?<P>Thanks,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000001||Old Lar||11-05-2000||09:47 AM||||Replace the stalk.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||11-05-2000||11:47 AM||||The switch in the column or the stalk could be worn. I expect the switch.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Bob Englert||11-05-2000||05:46 PM||||I don't mean to be preaching, but the stalk has NOTHING to do with the delay function of the wipers.  The stalk acts like a big stick that is jammed into the actual switch that you operate.  The actual action is controlled by the switch inside the column.  <P>I would only replace the stalk if it is physically broken so that the plastic delay portion is not operating the switch component in the column.  If the action is very touchy or there are lots of dead spots in the switch action, you will need to take the steering wheel off and remove several column comonents to replace the switch.  <P>I have used GP Sorensen #DS170 available from Advacne Auto or PartsAmerica.com with good sucess.<P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>87 Coupe<BR>85 SE (For Sale)||||reg||1
Z||000004||rockcrawl||11-05-2000||06:06 PM||||My car had a similar problem. The delay time was impossible to set, and the wipers would turn on whenever they wanted to. Using the turn signals almost always activated the wipers. Using the mist function would make the wipers run 3 or 4 times before stopping. I replaced the circuit board in the wiper motor housing and it now works perfectly. It's only one screw to change the board, so I'd try that first. Get one from a junk yard for a couple bucks.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT (awaiting new engine)<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<P>"Confidence is the feeling you have before you fully understand the situation"||||reg||1
